{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The HTML is the one file you must not cache long</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Don't put a long cache time on <code>index.html</code> itself, even though it feels consistent with "cache everything." It's the small file that has to be fetched fresh so the browser can learn the new hashed filenames after a deploy.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Use <code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code> (not <code>no-store</code>) on the HTML entry point: <code>no-cache</code> still lets the browser cache it but forces a revalidation request every time, which is cheap (a 304 response) and guarantees freshness.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Service workers complicate this further, a stale-while-revalidate SW cache can re-introduce the exact staleness problem hashed filenames were supposed to solve, since the SW's own cache lifecycle is independent of HTTP <code>Cache-Control</code> headers. If a service worker is in play, its update/activate lifecycle needs the same "small mutable pointer, large immutable payload" discipline applied deliberately, it doesn't inherit HTTP caching semantics for free.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

